Title: HARBOUR LIGHT
Credit: Written by
Author: Stripboard Test Fixture
Draft date: 2026-08-04

EXT. TRAWLER DECK - DAWN

Grey water slaps the hull. NELL OKONKWO, fifties, weatherbeaten, hauls a dripping net over the gunwale. A herring gull screams overhead.

The net comes up torn. She turns it over in her hands.

NELL
Third one this month.

INT. HARBOUR MASTER OFFICE - DAY

Charts cover every wall. TOMAS REYES pours coffee from a battered thermos and slides a clipboard across the desk.

TOMAS
Coastguard says the survey ship's been dragging gear off the point.

NELL
Then the coastguard can pay for my nets.

TOMAS
Sign here anyway. Paper beats shouting.

Nell signs. The pen leaks ink across her thumb.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE STAIRS - DUSK

Nell climbs the spiral iron stairs, a storm lantern swinging from one hand. Rain drives sideways through the broken window slats.

At the top she stops. A rusted padlock hangs open on the service hatch.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE LAMP ROOM - NIGHT

The great lens sits dark and dusty. A folding cot, a radio set and a scatter of tinned food fill the corner.

A young stowaway, MIRA, sixteen, presses herself against the curved glass, holding a fishing knife.

MIRA
I'll put it down if you put the lantern down.

Nell sets the lantern on the floor and raises both hands.

NELL
It's cold. There's soup in the office. Nobody's calling anybody.

Outside, a foghorn sounds twice.
